Track 2: Green Label, Black Oscillator
- Story & Context: Luc builds the EP’s starkest electronic groove at 110 BPM from a single square-wave oscillator, one dry kick, and a rim. Nadège assigns a bright, fine-seed kayamb to replace all conventional hats. Joël plays roulèr only on the second half of each two-bar cycle, creating a delayed low arrival. Rémi strikes a prepared reed-organ minor seventh once every four bars; uneven reed onset makes the chord sound slightly different each time. Anaïs uses bobre harmonics instead of full notes for the first half, then begins landing short bent fifths immediately after the synth bass cuts off. The track’s central contrast comes from an archive convention: every botanical specimen with a green label is dry-preserved, while Luc’s black oscillator never changes waveform. Organic classification and electronic limitation become parallel structures.
